But there are such goals.

Until I've found the vibrating square, every level
explored and survived is a goal.  When altar camping
getting books or AC points or artifacts or even crowned
is a goal that I can't complete in the hour or less I
get to play in a game (except the monthly train trip
to the downtown office when I get 90 minutes each way).
When I complete a big level like the Quest or Castle
shuttling the loot for polypiling is a multi-day goal.
When I complete Ludios shuttling the loot for polypiling
can be a couple of weeks worth of goal.

When playing to a goal, the goals just keep coming in
bite sized chunks.  One level at a time, one altar at
a time, one sacfest at a time, one shuttle cycle at a
time, one major level at a time, one unique item at a
time.

If you've never yet survived Medusa and you play to
goals, the major goal of your next character is to beat
medusa and the minor goals are one level at a time and
beating the other levels that appear before Medusa.
Move the experience level back far enough and the goal
becomes surviving your first foray into the mines.

Read posts for a while and you'll see a lot of players
set their goals this way.  { I'm deep in the maze levels
below that castle and I've reached a level without any
more down stairs and I can't even dig lower.  What's
going on? }  Someone who just there did it by setting
goals a level at a time and a puzzle at a time not by
reading in the Guidebook that ascension is the goal.



